F217 — a block-scope local shadowing an outer name is refused

    static constexpr uint64 tier = 1;
    locals.atOne = tier;            // "'tier' is used before its declaration"
    { uint64 tier = 20; ... }

Two diagnostics, one missing step: nothing ever resolved names against the block structure. The
backend allocates one wasm local per name per function, so the validator refused shadowing outright —
and because the flat set of locals is collected with the block structure discarded, a read that in
C++ unambiguously names an enclosing constant was also reported as a use before its declaration. That
is the hoist C99 block scoping exists to prevent.

Adds the resolution step, the standard way: alpha-renaming. Each function body is walked with a scope
stack; a declaration that reuses a name bound in an enclosing block — or, when nested, a name that
means something outside the function — is given a fresh name and the references that resolve to it
are rewritten. Downstream sees distinct names, so the one-slot-per-name model is correct without
changing it. A name with nothing to collide with keeps its spelling, which matters because the
backend recognises some macro-generated locals by name.

The declaration keeps the name as written, so a read of a block local from *outside* its block is
still reported; the use-before-declaration check now also lets a name that resolves outward through.
A synthetic compound (a multi-declarator statement) opens no scope. Two declarations of one name in
the same block stay a redeclaration.

Two tests change: both asserted that shadowing is rejected. The semantic-validation row's own comment
read "native keeps outer v = 1; the old single-slot lowering read back 2" — it is now an accepted row
with a toolchain test that runs it against clang and asserts 1.

Measured: BlockShadow both backends `1 20 300 1 1`, matching clang and core's WAMR host.
Suite 1085/0 (1084 + the new toolchain test).

